#import "void-import.typ": *

== Unramified, etale and smooth morphisms

#notation(name: <kahler-differentials>)[
  Let $f: X -> Y$ be locally of finite presentation between schemes. Then there is a sheaf $Omega_(X\/Y)$ of Kahler differentials, which is a finitely generated quasi-coherent $cal(O)_X$-module.
]

#example[
  Locally, let $X = Spec(B) -> Y = Spec(A)$, where $B = A[x_1,dots.c,x_n]\/I$. Then: 
  $
    Omega_(X\/Y) = tilde(Omega_(B\/A)), Omega_(B\/A) = (oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d x_i)/(gen(d f: f in I))
  $
  #example[
    If $B = A[x]\/(f(x))$, then: 
    $
      Omega_(B\/A) cong A[x]\/(f(x), f'(x))
    $
    #example(name: <rel-differential-of-fin-sep-ext>)[
      If $A = K$ is a field, and $B = K(alpha) = K[x]\/(f(x))$ is a finite simple extension, then: 
      $
        Omega_(B\/A) = 0 <==> (f(x), f'(x)) = (1) <==> K(alpha)\/K #[finite separable]
      $
      In particular, every finite separable field extension is simple by the primitive element theorem. Thus whenever $L\/K$ is a finite separable extension, $Omega_(L\/K) = 0$. 
    ]
  ]
]

#proposition(name: <equiv-defns-of-unramified>)[
  If $f: X -> Y$ is locally of finite presentation and $y = f(x)$, then TFAE: 
  + $cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)$ is a finite separable extension of $k(y)$. (In particular, $frak(m)_x = frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)$.)

  + $(Omega_(X\/Y))_x = 0$. 

  + The diagonal morphism $Delta_(X\/Y): X -> X times_Y X$ is an open immersion on an open neighborhood of $X$. 
]
#proof[
  This problem is local on base and source in nature, so we may assume that $X = Spec(B), Y = Spec(A)$ are connected, with $B$ a finitely generated $A$-algebra. Let $x$ correspond to $frak(q) subset B$, and $y$ correspond to $frak(p) subset A$.

  (2)$<==>$(3): 
  #proof[
    We first note that property (2) and (3) are both local in $x$. (3) is local in $x$ by definition, while (2) is local in $x$, since $Omega_(X\/Y)$ is a coherent sheaf on $X$ as $X -> Y$ is locally of finite type, and so it has closed support. As such, we may replace conditions (2)(3) with: 
    #[
      #set enum(numbering: "(1')")
      2. $Omega_(B\/A) = 0$; 
      3. $Delta_(X\/Y): X -> X times_Y X$ is an open immersion. 
    ]
    Let $I = ker(mu\: B otimes_A B -> B)$. Then since $Omega_(B\/A) = I\/I^2$ as $B$-modules, (2')$<==>$ $I = I^2$, while for a morphism between affine schemes, $Delta$ is automatically a closed immersion. Then (3')$<==>$ $Delta_(X\/Y)$ is an open immersion to some connected components of $X times_Y X$ $<==>$ $I = ker(mu)$ is generated by a single idempotent element. Thus, we need to prove that $I = I^2 <==> I = (e)$ where $e = e^2$. One implication is clear. 
    
    For the other direction, since $B\/A$ is finite type, $I$ is a finitely generated ideal. By Nakayama's lemma, there exists $e in I$ s.t. $(1-e)I = 0$. Then $(1-e)e = 0$, and so $e=e^2$ is idempotent, and $I = e I$ is generated by $e$. We are thereby done.  
    #suppress-qed
  ]
  (1)$<==>$(2): 
  #proof[
    Note that differential sheaves are preserved under base change:
    $
      Omega_(X\/Y) cong i_(X_y)^* (Omega_(X_y\/k(y)))
    $
    so:
    $
      (Omega_(X\/Y))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) cal(O)_(X_y,x) cong (Omega_(X_y\/k(y)))_x
    $
    Since $X$ and $X_y$ share the same residue field $k(x)$ at $x$, tensoring $k(x)$ to this equality gives:
    $
      (Omega_(X\/Y))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x) cong (Omega_(X_y\/k(y)))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X_y,x)) k(x)
    $
    Thus,
    $
      (Omega_(X\/Y))_x = 0 & <==> (Omega_(X\/Y))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x) = 0 "by Nakayama" \
                          & <==> (Omega_(X_y\/k(y)))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X_y,x)) k(x) = 0 \
                          & <==> (Omega_(X_y\/k(y)))_x = 0
    $
    Meanwhile, the condition (1) is also preserved under passing to fiber: Indeed, $cal(O)_(X_y,x) = cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)$ by construction, while the maximal ideal of a field is $0$. Thus, passing to the fiber, it suffices to show that if $f: X = Spec(A) -> Y = Spec(k)$ is of finite type, $x in X$, then TFAE:

    + $cal(O)_(X,x)$ is a field and a finite separable extension of $k$;

    + $(Omega_(X\/Y))_x = 0$.
    i.e. the original statement with $Y$ assumed to be spectrum of a field.
    
    Let $x$ correspond to the prime ideal $frak(q) subset A$. Now: 
    $
      (Omega_(X\/k))_x = Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A A_(frak(q)) = Omega_(A_(frak(q))\/k)
    $
    Thus, it suffices to prove that $Omega_(A_(frak(q))\/k) = 0$ if and only if $A_(frak(q))$ is a field and a finite separable field extension of $k$. The ($<==$) implication is clear from @rel-differential-of-fin-sep-ext, so we prove ($==>$) only. $Omega_(A\/k)$ is a finite $A$-module, so the vanishing of the stalk $Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A A_(frak(q))$ spreads out. Upon replacing $A$ by some $A_f$ for $f in A - frak(q)$, we may assume that $Omega_(A\/k) = 0$. In that case statement (1) states that $X = Spec(A)$ is artinian and reduced, hence $X = union.sq.big_i Spec(L_i)$ for finitely many finite separable extensions $L_i\/k$. 

    Consider first the case that $k$ is an algebraically closed field. By (2)$<==>$(3), $Omega_(A\/k)$ really says that the diagonal $Delta_(A\/k): Spec(A) -> Spec(A otimes_k A)$ is an open immersion. Since affine morphisms are closed, it is also a closed immersion. Thus for any closed point $x in X$, the graph $Gamma_x: Spec(k) -> Spec(k) times_k X$ of the inclusion $x: Spec(k) -> X$, being a base change of the diagonal, must be an open and closed immersion. Thus the inclusion $x -> X$ itself is an open and closed immersion. In other words, every closed point of $X$ is both open and closed. Thus $X$ is a disjoint union of its points. Finally, $X$ is reduced by the following lemma, and so a disjoint union of $Spec(k)$. We may thus conclude the proof in the case that $k$ is algebraically closed. 

    #lemma[
      For any artinian local ring $(A, frak(m))$ finite over an algebraically closed field $k$ s.t. $Omega_(A\/k) = 0$, $A = k$. 
    ]
    #proof[
      Let $g: k -> A$ be the structure map and $epsilon: A -> k$ be the reduction map. Define: 
      $
        delta: A -> frak(m)\/frak(m)^2, a |-> [a - g(epsilon(a))]
      $
      Then $delta(g(x)) = 0$ and: 
      $
        delta(a b) = [(a - g(epsilon(a))) b + g(epsilon(a)) (b - g(epsilon(b)))] = epsilon(a) delta(b) + epsilon(b) delta(a) in frak(m)\/frak(m)^2
      $
      Thus $delta$ is a $k$-derivation. However $Omega_(A\/k) = 0$ forces $delta = 0$. Thus for every $a in frak(m)$, $a = a - g(epsilon(a)) in frak(m)^2$ and so $frak(m) = frak(m)^2$. This forces $frak(m) = 0$ by Nakayama. 
    ]

    Now in the general case, $Omega_(A otimes_k ol(k)\/ol(k)) = Omega_(A\/k) otimes_k ol(k) = 0$. Thus $A otimes_k ol(k)$ is a finite product of $ol(k)$'s. In particular, by counting dimension, $A$ is finite over $k$, hence artinian. Since $A$ is geometrically reduced, it is reduced. Thus, 
    $
      Spec(A) = union.sq.big_i Spec(L_i)
    $
    for some finite field extensions $L_i\/k$. If $"char"(k) = 0$ we have separability automatically. If $"char"(k) = p$, take the separable closure $E_i$ of $k$ in $L_i$. Then $L_i = E_i [x_1, dots.c, x_m]\/(x_1^(p^(a_1)) - e_1, dots.c, x_m^(p^(a_m)) - e_m)$. Now $E_i otimes_k ol(k) = ol(k)^([E_i:k])$ by separability, so: 
    $
      L_i otimes_k ol(k) cong ((ol(k)[x_1, dots.c, x_m])/((x_1^(p^(a_1)) - e_1, dots.c, x_m^(p^(a_m)) - e_m)))^([E_i:k])
    $
    have components being nilpotent thickenings of $ol(k)$. Thus $L_i otimes_k ol(k)$ has $[E_i:k]$ connected components. On the other hand, $L_i otimes_k ol(k) cong ol(k)^([L_i:k])$ as we have shown. Thus $[E_i:k] = [L_i:k]$, $E_i = L_i$ and so $L_i\/k$ is separable. 
  ]
]

#definition(name: <defn-unramified-and-etale-morphisms>)[
  If $f: X -> Y$ is locally of finite presentation, we define: 
  // #set enum(numbering: "(a)")
  + $f$ is *unramified* at $x$ (in the sense of @cite-EGA4), if conditions (1)--(3) above hold for $x$.

  + $f$ is *etale* at $x$, if $f$ is unramified at $x$, and the local morphism $cal(O)_(Y,f(x)) -> cal(O)_(X,x)$ is flat (i.e. $f$ is flat at $x$). 

  + $f$ is *unramified/etale*, if it is so at every $x in X$. 

  #remark[
    Our definition of unramifiedness comes from @cite-SGA4. Some literature defines unramified morphisms to be locally of finite type + $Omega_(X\/Y) = 0$.

    This makes no difference when $X, Y$ are noetherian, but is slightly weaker than our definition which requires finite presentation. 
  ]
]

#properties(name: <etale-first-props>)[
  (First properties)

  0. Unramified/etale is local on base and source. 

  + Local immersions of finite presentation are unramified. Open immersions are etale. 

  + Unramified/etale morphisms are stable under compositions and base change. 

  + Given morphisms $X overset(->,f) Y overset(->,g) Z$, we have: 
    + $g compose f$ unramified $==>$ $f$ unramified; 
    + $g compose f$ etale and $g$ unramified $==>$ $f$ etale. 
]
#proof[
  (0) is automatic from the definition. 

  (2) Since flatness is preserved under base change and composition, it suffices to prove that unramifiedness is also preserved. By locality, we may assume that all schemes in question are affine. Stability under base change follows from the fact that: 
  $
    Omega_(B'\/A') cong Omega_(B\/A) otimes_A A' #[where] B' = B otimes_A A'
  $
  while stability under composition follows from the first exact sequence of Kahler differentials (@cite-matsumura-ca[Theorem 57]):
  $
    Omega_(B\/A) otimes_B C -> Omega_(C\/A) -> Omega_(C\/B) -> 0
  $
  where the first and third term vanishing implies vanishing of the second term. 

  (1) By locality and (2) it suffices to show that $id$ is etale and closed immersions are unramified. Obviously $id$ is etale. Closed immersions are unramified, since under the map $A ->> A\/I$, the multiplication map $mu: A\/I otimes_A A\/I -> A\/I$ is an isomorphism $A\/I otimes_A A\/I cong A\/I$. Its kernel is $0$, so $Omega_((A\/I)\/A) = 0$. 

  (3a) From locality, assume all schemes in question are affine. Consider the first exact sequence of Kahler differentials:
  $
    Omega_(B\/A) otimes_B C -> Omega_(C\/A) -> Omega_(C\/B) -> 0
  $
  The vanishing of the first and second term implies vanishing of the third. 

  (3b) The proof should seem automatic. You use base change and the graph scheme, similar to how you prove similar properties for separatedness and properness. Consider the Cartesian diagram: 
  // https://q.uiver.app/#r=typst&q=WzAsNCxbMCwwLCJYIl0sWzAsMiwiWSJdLFsyLDIsIlkgdGltZXNfWiBZIl0sWzIsMCwiWCB0aW1lc19aIFkiXSxbMCwxLCJmIl0sWzEsMiwiRGVsdGFfKFlcXC9aKSJdLFszLDIsImYgdGltZXMgaWRfWSIsMl0sWzAsMywiR2FtbWFfZiIsMl1d
  #align(center, diagram({
    node((-1, -1), [$X$])
    node((-1, 1), [$Y$])
    node((1, 1), [$Y times_Z Y$])
    node((1, -1), [$X times_Z Y$])
    edge((-1, -1), (-1, 1), [$f$], label-side: left, "->")
    edge((-1, 1), (1, 1), [$Delta_(Y\/Z)$], label-side: left, "->")
    edge((1, -1), (1, 1), [$f times id_Y$], label-side: right, "->")
    edge((-1, -1), (1, -1), [$Gamma_f$], label-side: right, "->")
  }))
  Since $g: Y -> Z$ is unramified, $Delta_(Y\/Z)$ is locally an open immersion, hence etale. Then the base change $Gamma_f$ is etale. Now $f$ factors as $X -->^(Gamma_f) X times_Z Y -->^(p_2) Y$. $p_2$ is the base change of $g compose f: X -> Z$, hence etale. Thus $f$ is the composition of two etale morphisms, hence etale. 
]

#example[
  (Examples of unramified maps)

  + Given $L\/K\/QQ$ finite extensions of $QQ$, 
    $
      f: Spec(cal(O)_L) -> Spec(cal(O)_K)
    $
    is flat. We want to check if it is unramified. $f$ is unramified at $frak(q)$, if for $frak(p) = frak(q) ∩ cal(O)_K$, $cal(O)_(L,frak(q))\/frak(p) cal(O)_(L,frak(q))$ is a finite separable extension of $cal(O)_K \/ frak(p)$, a finite field. 

    Every finite extension of a finite field is separable, so this is equivalent to $frak(p) cal(O)_(L,frak(q)) = frak(q) cal(O)_(L,frak(q))$, i.e. $e(frak(q)\/frak(p)) = 1$, i.e. $frak(q)$ is unramified above $frak(p)$ in the sense of places of number fields. 

  + Let $X = Spec(B) cong AA_k^1$, $B = k[x]$, and $Y = Spec(A) cong AA_k^1$, $A = k[y]$. Define $f: X -> Y$ by $k[y] -> k[x], y |-> x^n$, the $n$-th power map, where $n >= 2$. Since $B$ is a finite free $A$-module, $f$ is flat. Now: 
    $
      Omega_(B\/A) = (B d x)/((n x^(n-1) d x)) cong k[x]/((n x^(n-1)))
    $
    Thus, if $n in k^times$, then $f$ is etale at $(x-a)$ for any $a != 0 in k$. 

    In any case, as long as $n>=2$, $f$ is not etale at $(x)$. 
]

#example(name: <kummer-and-artin-schreier-coverings>)[
  (Examples of etale maps) Two of the most important examples of etale maps, or more precisely finite etale maps, are the *Kummer covering* and *Artin-Schreier covering*. 

  + (Kummer covering) Let $X$ be a scheme s.t. $n in cal(O)_X^times$. For a function $x in cal(O)_X (X)^times$, the Kummer covering is defined as: 
    $
      tilde(X) = SPec((cal(O)_X [t])/((t^n - x))) -> X
    $
    It is locally free, hence flat. Locally, it looks like $Spec(A[t]\/(t^n - x)) -> Spec(A)$. Thus locally $Omega_(tilde(X)\/X)$ looks like:
    $
      (A[t] d t)/((t^n - x, n t^(n-1))) = 0      
    $
    since $n in A^times$ and $x in A^times$. Thus $tilde(X) -> X$ is unramified. It is thus etale. 

  + (Artin-Schreier covering) Let $X$ be an $FF_p$-scheme. For a function $x in cal(O)_X (X)$, the Artin-Schreier covering is defined as: 
    $
      tilde(X) = SPec((cal(O)_X [t])/((t^p - t - x))) -> X
    $
    It is locally free, hence flat. Locally, it looks like $Spec(A[t]\/(t^p - t - x)) -> Spec(A)$. Thus locally $Omega_(tilde(X)\/X)$ looks like:
    $
      (A[t] d t)/((t^p - t - x, -1)) = 0      
    $
    Thus $tilde(X) -> X$ is unramified, hence etale. 
]

#proposition[
  Etale morphisms are open and quasi-finite. 
]
#proof[
  Etale morphisms are open since flat morphisms are open. (This can be checked in the noetherian case as in @cite-hartshorne-ag[Exercise III.9.1]. In the general case, pass to affine opens and use passage to limit as in @absolute-noetherian-approx and @passage-to-limit-of-properties (a) to reduce to the noetherian case.)
  
  They are quasi-finite, since their fibers are etale. However if $k$ is a field, by definition of unramifiedness, any unramified $k$-scheme $X$ must look like: 
  $
    X = union.sq.big_i Spec(L_i), L_i\/k #[finite separable]
  $
  which is discrete. Thus every etale morphism has discrete fibers and is quasi-finite. 
]

Next, we prove the permanence of many desirable properties under etale morphisms. 

#theorem(name: <etale-local-properties>)[
  Let $f: X -> Y$ be a morphism etale at $x$ with $y = f(x)$. Then any of the following properties holds for $cal(O)_(X,x)$ if and only if the same property holds for $cal(O)_(Y,y)$: 

  0. noetherian (in which case $dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(cal(O)_(Y,y))$); 
  + regular; 
  + has Serre's property $(R_k)$;
  + has Serre's property $(S_k)$; 
  + reduced; 
  + normal. 
]
#proof[
  We first prove a lemma governing many converse statements. 

  #lemma(name: <noetherianity-normality-descends-along-faithfully-flat-map>)[
    Let $A -> B$ be a faithfully flat morphism. Then: 

    + $A$ is noetherian if $B$ is noetherian; 

    + $A$ is normal if $B$ is normal. 
  ]
  #proof[
    (1) For any ideal $I subset A$, $I B subset B$ is finitely generated, say by $a_1, dots.c, a_n in I$. Then by faithful flatness, 
    $
      I = I B inter A = (a_1, dots.c, a_n) B inter A = (a_1, dots.c, a_n)
    $
    and so $I$ is finitely generated. 

    (2) If $B$ is normal, since $A -> B$ is injective, $A$ is at least integral. Now for any $a\/b in Frac(A)$ integral over $A$, it is integral over $B$ as an element of $Frac(B)$, and so $a\/b in B$. Thus $a in A inter b B$. But by faithful flatness, $A inter b B = b A$, and so $a\/b in A$. Thus $A$ is normal. 
  ]

  Base changing $f$ along $Spec(cal(O)_(Y,y)) -> Y$ does not affect local rings at $x$ and $y$. Thus performing this base change and then shrinking $X$ around $x$, we may assume that $f: Spec(B) = X -> Y = Spec(A)$ is etale between two affine schemes, with $A$ local and $y$ the closed point of $Spec(A)$. 

  (0) If $cal(O)_(Y,y)$ is noetherian, $cal(O)_(X,x)$, being essentially of finite presentation over $cal(O)_(Y,y)$, must be noetherian. The converse direction is governed by @noetherianity-normality-descends-along-faithfully-flat-map (1) since $cal(O)_(Y,y) -> cal(O)_(X,x)$ is faithfully flat. 
  
  Suppose that $cal(O)_(X,x)$ is noetherian. For any ideal $I subset cal(O)_(Y,y)$, $I cal(O)_(X,x)$ is finitely generated as an ideal of $cal(O)_(X,x)$, say by $f_1, dots.c, f_n in I$. Since $cal(O)_(Y,y) -> cal(O)_(X,x)$ is faithfully flat, $I = cal(O)_(Y,y) inter I cal(O)_(X,x) = cal(O)_(Y,y) inter (f_1, dots.c, f_n) cal(O)_(X,x) = (f_1, dots.c, f_n)$. Thus $I$ is finitely generated. This proves that $cal(O)_(Y,y)$ is noetherian. 
  
  For dimensions, since $cal(O)_(Y,y) -> cal(O)_(X,x)$ is flat, by #cite-stacks("00ON")[Lemma 10.112.7], 
  $
    dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(cal(O)_(Y,y)) + dim(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x))
  $
  By etaleness, $cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x) cong k(x)$ is zero-dimensional. Thus $dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(cal(O)_(Y,y))$. A similar formula exists for depth by #cite-stacks("0337")[Lemma 10.163.2], 
  $
    "depth"(cal(O)_(X,x)) = "depth"(cal(O)_(Y,y)) + "depth"(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)) = "depth"(cal(O)_(Y,y))
  $
  where $"depth"(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)) = "depth"(k(x)) = 0$. 

  For (1)(2)(3), by (0) we may assume that both $cal(O)_(X,x)$ and $cal(O)_(Y,y)$ are noetherian and have the same dimension $d$. 

  (1) Suppose that $f_1, dots.c, f_t$ is a minimal set of generators of $frak(m)_y$. By etaleness, $frak(m)_x = frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)$. Thus $f_1, dots.c, f_t$ is also a minimal set of generators of $frak(m)_x$. Hence, 
  $
    dim_(k(y)) (frak(m)_y\/frak(m)_y^2) = t = dim_(k(x)) (frak(m)_x\/frak(m)_x^2)
  $
  Hence regularity of either $cal(O)_(X,x)$ or $cal(O)_(Y,y)$ is equivalent to $t = d$, thus equivalent to each other. 

  (2) For any $frak(q) in Spec(cal(O)_(X,x))$ lying above $frak(p) in Spec(A)$, by (0), 
  $
    dim(B_(frak(q))) = dim(A_(frak(p)))
  $
  so $dim(B_(frak(q))) <= k$ if and only if $dim(A_(frak(p))) <= k$. Also by (1) the regularity of $B_(frak(q))$ and $A_(frak(p))$ are equivalent. Thus, $cal(O)_(X,x)$ is ($R_k$) if and only if $A$ is. 

  (3) For any $frak(q) in Spec(cal(O)_(X,x))$ lying above $frak(p) in Spec(A)$, by the dimension and depth formulas, 
  $
    "depth"(B_(frak(q))) = "depth"(A_(frak(p)))
  $
  and: 
  $
    min(k, dim(B_(frak(q)))) = min(k, dim(A_(frak(p))))
  $
  Thus both LHSs are equal if and only if both RHSs are equal. Apply this for all $frak(q)$, and we see that $cal(O)_(X,x)$ is ($S_k$) if and only if $A$ is. 

  For (4)(5), in the case that $A = cal(O)_(Y,y)$ is noetherian, we have Serre's criteria: 

  - reducedness $<==> (R_0) and (S_1)$; 
  - normality $<==> (R_1) and (S_2)$. 

  Thus (2)(3) implies (4)(5) in the noetherian case. In general, we need to use limit arguments. Write $A = dirlim_lambda A_lambda$, where each $A_lambda$ is obtained from a finite type $ZZ$-subalgebra $A_(lambda,0)$ of $A$ localized w.r.t. $A_(lambda,0) inter frak(m)_A$, ordered under inclusion. By @passage-to-limit-of-morphisms (1), there exists a finite stage $lambda_0$ and an $A_(lambda_0)$-algebra $B_(lambda_0)$ s.t. $B cong B_(lambda_0) otimes_(A_(lambda_0)) A$. Put $B_lambda = B_(lambda_0) otimes_(A_(lambda_0)) A_lambda$. By @passage-to-limit-of-properties (3d), up to enlarging $lambda_0$, we may assume that $B_(lambda_0)$ is an etale $A_(lambda_0)$-algebra. Thus for each $lambda >= lambda_0$, $B_lambda$ is an etale $A_lambda$-algebra. 
  
  Since $B = B_lambda otimes_(A_lambda) A$ and $B_lambda$ is flat over $A_lambda$, the maps $B_lambda -> B$ are also inclusions. So we have directed limits: 
  $
    A = dirlim_lambda A_lambda, B = dirlim_lambda B_lambda 
  $
  with injective transition maps. Let $x in X$ correspond to a prime $frak(q) in Spec(B)$, pulling back to $frak(q)_lambda in Spec(B_lambda)$. Then: 
  $
    B_(frak(q)) = dirlim_lambda B_(lambda,frak(q)_lambda)
  $
  also has injective transition maps. Now: 

  - For reducedness, $A$ is reduced if and only if every $A_lambda$ is reduced (since the nilpotents of $A$ are just the union of the nilpotents of every $A_lambda$). Similarly, $B$ is reduced if and only if every $B_(lambda,frak(q)_lambda)$ is reduced. Thus (4) follows from the noetherian case. 

  - For normality, one direction follows from a limit argument. Suppose that $A$ is normal. Then by replacing each $A_lambda$ (known to be integral) with its integral closure in $A$, we may assume that every $A_lambda$ is normal. Then by the noetherian case of (5), every $B_(lambda,frak(q)_lambda)$ is normal. Thus it is easy to check that the limit $B_(frak(q))$ is normal. 

  - The other direction for normality can be checked directly. Note that $A -> C = B_(frak(q))$ is faithfully flat. Thus if $C$ is normal, @noetherianity-normality-descends-along-faithfully-flat-map (2) may be invoked to conclude that $A$ is normal. 

  We have thus proved (4)(5) in the general case. 
]

Etaleness can be obtained using Jacobians, as in the following lemma. 
#lemma(name: <jacobian-criterion-for-etaleness>)[
  (Jacobian criterion for etaleness) Let: 
  $
    B = A[T_1, dots.c, T_n]/((F_1, dots.c, F_n))
  $
  so that the Jacobian matrix $J = display(mat(nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_n))$ is invertible in $B$. Then $B$ is etale over $A$. 
]
#proof[
  Firstly, 
  $
    Omega_(B\/A) = (oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i)/((d F_1, dots.c, d F_n)) = 0
  $
  so $B$ is unramified over $A$. Thus, it suffices to establish flatness. 
  // We may pick a subring $A_0 subset A$ that is finite type $ZZ$-algebra s.t. every $F_i in A_0 [T_1, dots.c, T_n]$. Put: 
  // $
  //   B_0 = (A_0 [T_1, dots.c, T_n])/((F_1, dots.c, F_n))
  // $
  // By enlarging $A_0$ so that $B_0$ contains the inverse of the determinant of the Jacobian $J = display(mat(nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_n))$, we may assume that $J$ is invertible in $B_0$. Thus replacing $A, B$ with $A_0, B_0$, we may assume that $A, B$ are noetherian. 
  Let's first assume that $A$ is noetherian. 
  
  Fix $frak(q) in Spec(B)$ lying above $frak(p) in Spec(A)$. Since the flat locus is open, it suffices to prove flatness at a dense subset of points. Thus we may assume that $frak(q)$ is a closed point in its fiber over $frak(p)$. Let $P = A[T_1,dots.c,T_n]$ and let $frak(r) in Spec(P)$ correspond to $frak(q)$. We aim to show that $A_(frak(p)) -> B_(frak(q))$ is flat. Note that $B_(frak(q)) = P_(frak(r))\/(F_1, dots.c, F_n)$ and $A_(frak(p)) -> P_(frak(r))$ is flat. Thus, we want to use the following flatness criterion: 

  #lemma[
    (#cite-stacks("00MG")[Lemma 10.99.3]) Suppose that $A -> B$ is a flat local homomorphism of noetherian local rings, and $f_1, dots.c, f_c in B$ s.t. $[f_1], dots.c, [f_c] in B\/frak(m)_A B$ is a regular sequence. Then $B\/(f_1, dots.c, f_c)$ is flat over $A$.  
  ]

  By this criterion, note that:
  $
    P_(frak(r))\/frak(p) P_(frak(r)) = k(frak(p))[T_1, dots.c, T_n]_(ol(frak(r)))
  $
  for $ol(frak(r))$ the reduction of $frak(r)$. Since $frak(q)$ is a closed point of its fiber over $frak(p)$, $ol(frak(r))$ is a maximal ideal of $S = k(frak(p))[T_1, dots.c, T_n]$. Thus RHS is a regular local ring of dimension $n$. 
  
  Thus it suffices to prove that the $n$ elements $ol(F)_1, dots.c, ol(F)_n in S = k(frak(p))[T_1, dots.c, T_n]$ forms a regular sequence in this $n$-dimensional regular local ring $S_(ol(frak(r)))$. Since $F_1, dots.c, F_n in frak(r)$, $ol(F)_1, dots.c, ol(F)_n in ol(frak(r)) S_(ol(frak(r)))$. 
  
  Now it suffices to show that $ol(F)_1, dots.c, ol(F)_n in ol(frak(r)) S_(ol(frak(r)))$ are linearly independent in $ol(frak(r)) S_(ol(frak(r)))\/ol(frak(r))^2 S_(ol(frak(r)))$. Consider the cotangent map: 
  $
    ol(frak(r)) S_(ol(frak(r)))\/ol(frak(r))^2 S_(ol(frak(r))) &-> Omega_(S_(ol(frak(r)))\/k(frak(p))) \
    [F] &|-> d F
  $
  which sends: 
  $
    ol(F)_i |-> sum_(j=1)^n (partial ol(F)_i)/(partial T_j) d T_j
  $
  Since the Jacobian matrix $mat(display((partial ol(F)_i)/(partial T_j)))$ is a reduction of $J$, it is invertible. Thus $d ol(F)_i$ are linearly independent over $k(frak(p))$ in $Omega_(S_(ol(frak(r)))\/k(frak(p)))$. They are thus linearly independent in $ol(frak(r)) S_(ol(frak(r)))\/ol(frak(r))^2 S_(ol(frak(r)))$, and hence form a regular sequence for $S_(ol(frak(r)))$. We have thereby proved the case where $A$ is noetherian. 

  For the general case, pick a finite type $ZZ$-subalgebra $A_0 subset A$ s.t. every $F_i in A_0 [T_1, dots.c, T_n]$. Furthermore, there exists $G_0, G_1, dots.c, G_n in A_0 [T_1, dots.c, T_n]$ s.t: 
  $
    G_0 det(J) + sum_(i=1)^n G_i F_i = 1
  $
  Then $B_0 = A_0 [T_1,dots.c,T_n]\/(F_1,dots.c,F_n)$ also satisfies the hypothesis, with $A_0$ noetherian. Thus $B_0$ is etale over $A_0$. Now $B = B_0 otimes_(A_0) A$ is etale over $A$. 
]

Next, we need to define smooth morphisms. We need to establish the equivalences of various definitions of smoothness. For this we need some lemmas. 

#lemma(name: <equiv-defns-of-smooth-auxiliary>)[
  Let $X$ be a finite type scheme over a field $k$ and $x in X$. Then: 
  $
    dim((Omega_(X\/k))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x)) >= d_x := dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) + trd_k (k(x))
  $
  Here, RHS is also the largest dimension of the irreducible components of $X$ containing $x$. 

  Furthermore, equality holds if and only if $X$ is geometrically regular in a neighborhood around $x$. In that case, $Omega_(X\/k)$ is locally free around $x$. For any local presentation $X = Spec(A)$ for $A = k[T_1, dots.c, T_n]\/I$, there exist elements $F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x) in I$ that generate $I$ around $x$, and coordinates $T_(j_1), dots.c, T_(j_(d_x))$ s.t. the Jacobian matrix $J = display(mat(nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_(n-d_x), E_(j_1), dots.c, E_(j_(d_x)))) in MM_(n times n) (k[T_1, dots.c, T_n])$ is invertible upon reduction to $A$. 
]
#proof[
  This lemma is local around $x$, so we may shrink $X$ around $x$ to make $X = Spec(A)$ affine. Let $x$ correspond to a prime ideal $frak(p)$, so $k(x) = A_(frak(p))\/frak(p) A_(frak(p))$. Let $phi_x: A -> k(x)$ be the evaluation map. Now put: 
  $
    psi_x: A otimes_k ol(k(x)) -> ol(k(x)), a otimes u |-> phi_x (a) u
  $
  Let $frak(q) = ker(psi_x)$ and $S = (A otimes_k ol(k(x)))_(frak(q))$. Then $S_(frak(q))\/frak(q) S_(frak(q)) = ol(k(x))$. Further, 

  - $frak(q) in Spec(S)$ lies above $frak(p) in Spec(A)$. Furthermore, extension of base field does not change the dimensions of irreducible components. Thus, 
    $
      dim(S_(frak(q))) = max_(Z subset Spec(S) \ #[irreducible component]) dim(Z) = max_(Z subset Spec(A) \ #[irreducible component]) dim(Z) = d_x
    $

  - Meanwhile, $frak(q) in Spec(S)$ is a $ol(k(x))$-rational point. Now: 
    $
      Omega_(S\/ol(k(x))) = Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A S
    $
    Localizing at $frak(q)$, we see that: 
    $
      Omega_(S_(frak(q))\/ol(k(x))) = Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A S_(frak(q))
    $
    and finally, reducing modulo $frak(q) S_(frak(q))$ yields: 
    $
      Omega_(S_(frak(q))\/ol(k(x))) otimes_(S_(frak(q))) ol(k(x)) &= Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A ol(k(x)) = Omega_(A_(frak(p))\/k) otimes_(A_(frak(p))) ol(k(x)) \
      &= (Omega_(A_(frak(p))\/k) otimes_(A_(frak(p))) k(x)) otimes_(k(x)) ol(k(x))
    $
    Thus, 
    $
    dim_(ol(k(x))) (Omega_(S_(frak(q))\/ol(k(x))) otimes_(S_(frak(q))) ol(k(x))) = dim_(k(x)) (Omega_(A_(frak(p))\/k) otimes_(A_(frak(p))) k(x))
    $
  On the other hand, $frak(q) in Spec(S)$ is a rational point of the finite type $ol(k(x))$-scheme $Spec(S)$. By @cite-hartshorne-ag[Proposition III.8.6], $Omega_(S_(frak(q))\/ol(k(x))) cong frak(q) S_(frak(q))\/frak(q)^2 S_(frak(q))$. Thus, our desired inequality becomes:
  $
    dim_(ol(k(x))) (frak(q) S_(frak(q))\/frak(q)^2 S_(frak(q))) >= dim(S_(frak(q)))
  $
  which is the standard inequality comparing a local ring's dimension with the minimal number of generators for its maximal ideal. Furthermore, if $Spec(A)$ is geometrically regular around $x$, then $S_(frak(q))$ would be regular, causing the inequality above to be an equality. Conversely, pick a presentation: 
  $
    A = P\/I, P = k[T_1, dots.c, T_n]
  $
  and let $frak(q) in Spec(P)$ be the prime ideal that $frak(p) in Spec(A)$ corresponds to. We now have the second exact sequence (@cite-matsumura-ca[Theorem 58]): 
  $
    I\/I^2 -> Omega_(P\/k) otimes_P A -> Omega_(A\/k) -> 0
  $
  Tensor it with $k(x)$: 
  $
    I\/I^2 otimes_A k(x) -> oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(x) d T_i -> Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A k(x) -> 0
  $
  In the case of equality, $dim_(k(x)) (Omega_(A\/k) otimes_A k(x)) = d_x$. Thus $dim_(k(x)) (im(I\/I^2 otimes_A k(x)-> oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(x) d T_i)) = n - d_x$. We may thus pick $F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x) in I$ s.t. ${d F_i}$ forms a basis of $im(I\/I^2 otimes_A k(x)-> oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(x) d T_i)$. Now:
  $
    dim(P_(frak(q))) = dim(P) - dim(P\/frak(q)) = n - trd_k (Frac(P\/frak(q))) = n - trd_k (k(x))
  $
  The classes of $F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)$ in $frak(q) P_(frak(q))\/frak(q)^2 P_(frak(q))$ are $k(x)$-linearly independently, for otherwise they would induce a $k(x)$-linear relation between $d F_1, dots.c, d F_(n-d_x)$. Thus, $F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)$ extends to a regular system of parameters for the regular local ring $P_(frak(q))$, and so $P_(frak(q))\/(F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x))$ is a regular local ring of dimension: 
  $
    dim(P_(frak(q))\/(F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x))) = dim(P_(frak(q))) - (n - d_x) = d_x - trd_k (k(x)) = dim(cal(O)_(X,x))
  $
  On the other hand, it surjects onto $P_(frak(q))\/I P_(frak(q)) = cal(O)_(X,x)$, also of dimension $dim(cal(O)_(X,x))$. A proper quotient of a regular local ring must have a smaller dimension. Thus, it has to be that: 
  $
    I P_(frak(q)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)) subset P_(frak(q))
  $
  Now pick coordinates $T_(j_1), dots.c, T_(j_(d_x))$ s.t. $[d T_(j_i)]$ forms a basis of $coker(I\/I^2 otimes_A k(x)-> oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(x) d T_i)$. Then $d F_1, dots.c, d F_(n-d_x), d T_(j_1), dots.c, d T_(j_(d_x))$ forms a basis of $oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(x) d T_i$. 

  Form the Jacobian matrix: 
  $
    J = mat(nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_(n-d_x), E_(j_1), dots.c, E_(j_(d_x))) in MM_(n times n) (P)
  $
  where $E_j$ is the $j$-th standard basis vector. By our discussions above, $det(J) in P$ reduces to a unit in $k(x)$, hence a unit in $A_(frak(p))$. By spreading out, $det(J)$ is a unit in $A_h$ for some $h in P$. Since $I P_(frak(q)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)) subset P_(frak(q))$, by shrinking $Spec(P_h)$ around $frak(q)$, we may assume that $I P_h = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)) subset P_h$. We now claim that $Spec(A_h)$ is geometrically regular. Indeed, 
  $
    A_h = P_h\/I P_h = P_h\/(F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x)) = k[T_0, T_1, dots.c, T_n]\/(T_0 h - 1, F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d_x))
  $
  Form the Jacobian matrix: 
  $
    J' &= mat(nabla (T_0 h - 1), nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_(n-d_x), E_(j_1), dots.c, E_(j_(d_x))) \
    &= mat(h, 0; T_0 nabla h, J) in MM_((n+1) times (n+1)) (P[T_0]) 
  $
  which is invertible in $A_h$ (since $h$ and $det(J)$ are invertible). Expanding the determinant, this says that the Jacobian $display(mat(nabla (T_0 h - 1), nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_(n-d_x)))$ of this presentation has an $(1+n-d_x) times (1+n-d_x)$ invertible minor by deleting rows $j_1, dots.c, j_(d_x)$. This minor remains invertible upon any extension of the base field $k$. Thus to prove geometric regularity, it remains to prove: 

  #lemma(name: <jacobian-criterion-for-geom-regularity>)[
    Let $A$ be a finite type $k$-algebra with presentation $A cong k[T_1, dots.c, T_n]\/(f_1, dots.c, f_m)$, $m <= n$. Suppose that the Jacobian $J = display(mat(nabla f_1, dots.c, nabla f_m))$ has an invertible $m times m$ minor. Then $A$ is geometrically regular over $k$. 
  ]
  #proof[
    Put $P = k[T_1, dots.c, T_n], I = (f_1, dots.c, f_m)$. Then form the second exact sequence: 
    $
      I\/I^2 -> Omega_(P\/k) otimes_P A -> Omega_(A\/k) -> 0
    $
    By the invertible $m times m$ minor, $d f_1, dots.c, d f_m$ span a free submodule of $Omega_(P\/k) otimes_P A cong oplus.big_(i=1)^n A d T_i$ that has rank $m$. Thus for every point $frak(p) in Spec(A)$, $d f_1, dots.c, d f_m$ span an $m$-dimensional subspace of $oplus.big_(i=1)^n k(frak(p)) d T_i$
    
    Now for every closed point $frak(p) in Spec(A)$ coming from a closed point $frak(q) in Spec(P)$, $A_(frak(p)) = P_(frak(q))\/(f_1, dots.c, f_m)$. The classes of $f_1, dots.c, f_m$ in $frak(q) P_(frak(q))\/frak(q)^2 P_(frak(q))$ are linearly independent, for otherwise they would define a $k(frak(p))$-linear relation between $d f_1, dots.c, d f_m$. Thus $f_1, dots.c, f_m$ extend to a regular system of parameters of the $n$-dimensional regular local ring $P_(frak(q))$. Hence $A_(frak(p)) = P_(frak(q))\/(f_1, dots.c, f_m)$ is regular. The same argument applies after every field extension of $k$, because the Jacobian minor remains invertible; hence $A$ is geometrically regular over $k$. 
  ]

  For local freeness of $Omega_(A\/k)$, for such a presentation, 
  $
    Omega_(A\/k) = (A d T_1 oplus dots.c oplus A d T_n)/((d f_1, dots.c, d f_m))
  $
  is locally free over $A$ of rank $n - m$ by the invertible Jacobian minor. 
]

#proposition(name: <equiv-defns-of-smooth>)[
  Let $f: X -> Y$ be a morphism locally of finite presentation. For $y = f(x)$, TFAE: 

  + $f$ is flat at $x$ and: #h(1fr)
    $
      dim_(k(x)) ((Omega_(X\/Y))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x)) = dim(cal(O)_(X_y,x)) + trd_(k(y)) (k(x))
    $
    where RHS is also the dimension of a sufficiently small open neighborhood of $x in X_y$. The equality here may be weakened to a $<=$. 

  + $f$ is flat at $x$, and $X_y$ is geometrically regular around $x$. 

  + There exists an open neighborhood $x in U subset X$ and a factorization $f|_U: U ->^j AA^d_Y -> Y$ in which $j$ is etale. 

  + For any affine opens $x in Spec(B) subset X$, $y in Spec(A) subset Y$ and any presentation $B cong P\/I, P = A[T_1, dots.c, T_n]$, the second exact sequence: 
    $
      I\/I^2 -> Omega_(P\/A) otimes_P B -> Omega_(B\/A) -> 0
    $
    a priori right exact, is actually split exact on stalks at $x$. 

  + For some affine opens $x in Spec(B) subset X$, $y in Spec(A) subset Y$ and some presentation $B cong P\/I, P = A[T_1, dots.c, T_n]$, the second exact sequence: 
    $
      I\/I^2 -> Omega_(P\/A) otimes_P B -> Omega_(B\/A) -> 0
    $
    a priori right exact, is actually split exact on stalks at $x$. 
  
  Under these conditions, $Omega_(X\/Y)$ is locally free at $x$ of rank $d$. 
]
#proof[
  @equiv-defns-of-smooth-auxiliary immediately implies (1)$<==>$(2), since: 
  $
    (Omega_(X\/Y))_x otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x) &cong Omega_(cal(O)_(X,x)\/cal(O)_(Y,y)) otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x) \
    &cong Omega_(cal(O)_(X,x)\/cal(O)_(Y,y)) otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)) cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x) otimes_(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)) k(x) \
    &cong Omega_(cal(O)_(X_y,x)\/k(y)) otimes_(cal(O)_(X_y,x)) k(x)
  $

  Next, we prove (3)$==>$(2). Indeed, by shrinking $X$ around $x$, we may factor: 
  $
    f: X ->^j AA^d_Y -> Y
  $
  for $j$ etale. Then for any field extension $L\/k(y)$, we have a map on fiber $(X_y)_L -> AA^d_L -> L$ with $(X_y)_L\/AA^d_L$ etale. Since $AA^d_L$ is regular, $(X_y)_L$ is regular by @etale-local-properties (1). Thus $X_y$ is geometrically regular. Flatness follows from etaleness. 

  Now we prove (1)(2)$==>$(3), thus establishing the equivalence of (1)(2)(3). 
  
  #proof[
    All concerned properties are local around $x$ and $y$, so we may assume that $Y = Spec(A)$ with $y$ corresponding to a prime $frak(p)$, and $X = Spec(B)$ with $x$ corresponding to $frak(q)$. Put $ol(B) = B_(frak(p))\/frak(p) B_(frak(p))$ so that $X_y = Spec(ol(B))$ and let $ol(frak(q)) in Spec(ol(B))$ correspond to $frak(q)$. 
  
    By @equiv-defns-of-smooth-auxiliary, $Omega_(ol(B)\/k(y))$ is locally free of rank $d$ at $ol(frak(q))$, where up to shrinking $B$ around $ol(frak(q))$, $ol(B)$ is geometrically regular over $k(y)$ of dimension $d$, and has connected spectrum. In particular, at least $ol(B)$ is integral regular of dimension $d$. 
    
    Pick $ol(b)_i in ol(B)_(ol(frak(q)))$ s.t. $[d ol(b)_1], dots.c, [d ol(b)_d]$ forms a basis of $Omega_(ol(B)_(ol(frak(q)))\/k(y)) otimes_(ol(B)_(ol(frak(q)))) k(x)$. Now by Nakayama's lemma, $Omega_(ol(B)_(ol(frak(q))\/k(y))$ is generated by the elements $d ol(b)_1, dots.c, d ol(b)_d$. Next, arbitrarily lift $ol(b)_i$ to $b_i in B_(frak(q))$, and: 
    $
      Omega_(ol(B)_(ol(frak(q)))\/k(y)) = Omega_(B_(frak(q))\/A_(frak(p))) otimes_(B_(frak(q))) B_(frak(q))\/frak(p) B_(frak(q))
    $
    is generated by the elements $[d b_1], dots.c, [d b_d]$. Now $frak(p) B_(frak(q)) subset frak(q) B_(frak(q))$, so by Nakayama's lemma, $Omega_(B_(frak(q))\/A_(frak(p)))$ is generated by the elements $d b_1, dots.c, d b_d$. In other words, $(Omega_(B\/A))_(frak(q))$ is generated by $d b_1, dots.c, d b_d$. 
    
    Now $b_i in B_(frak(q))$, so by shrinking $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$, we may assume that each $b_i in B$ is a global section. Thus these sections define a map: 
    $
      phi: P = A[T_1, dots.c, T_d] -> B
    $
    by evaluation, thus defining a factorization $Spec(B) -> Spec(P) = AA^d_A -> Spec(A)$. Consider the first exact sequence of the relative differentials: 
    $
      Omega_(P\/A) otimes_P B -> Omega_(B\/A) -> Omega_(B\/P) -> 0
    $
    The first map sends $d T_i |-> d b_i$. Thus, $Omega_(P\/A) otimes_P B -> Omega_(B\/A)$ is surjective upon localizing $B$ at $frak(q)$. Shrinking $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$, we may assume that this map is surjective. Thus $Omega_(B\/P) = 0$, and so $B$ is unramified over $P$. Shrinking $B$ around $frak(q)$ further, we may assume that $B$ is flat over $A$. 

    #lemma[
      Let $k$ be a field and $X, Y$ be two connected regular finite type $k$-schemes of the same dimension, with an unramified map $f: X -> Y$. Then $f$ is etale. 
    ]
    #proof[
      $X, Y$ are integral as regular connected noetherian schemes. //Since $f$ is dominant, the generic point of $X$ lies above the generic point of $Y$, so $K(X)\/K(Y)$ is a finite separable extension. Thus $dim(X) = trd_k (K(X)) = trd_k (K(Y)) = dim(Y)$. 
      
      For any closed point $x in X$, $y = f(x) in Y$ is a closed point. Now $cal(O)_(X,x), cal(O)_(Y,y)$ are both regular local rings. By miracle flatness (#cite-stacks("00R4")[Lemma 10.128.1]), it suffices to check: 
      $
        dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(cal(O)_(Y,y)) + dim(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x))
      $
      By unramifiedness $dim(cal(O)_(X,x)\/frak(m)_y cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(k(x)) = 0$. Since $x, y$ are closed points of $X, Y$, 
      $
        dim(cal(O)_(X,x)) = dim(X) = dim(Y) = dim(cal(O)_(Y,y))
      $
      We have thus shown that $f$ is flat at every closed point of $X$. Since the flat locus is open, $f$ is flat. 
    ]

    Now: 

    - $B$ is flat over $A$ as assumed, and $P = A[T_1,dots.c,T_d]$ is flat over $A$. 

    - The fiber map $k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_d] -> ol(B)$ is known to be unramified. $ol(B)$ is assumed to be integral regular of dimension $d$. $k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_d]$ is of course integral regular. Now: 

      $
        dim(ol(B)) = d = dim(k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_d])
      $

      Thus by the previous lemma, $k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_d] -> ol(B)$ is etale. 

    Thus by the fiberwise criterion for flatness (#cite-stacks("039C")[Theorem 37.16.2]), $B$ is flat over $P$ at $frak(q)$. Shrinking $B$ around $frak(q)$ once more, $Spec(B) -> AA^d_A$ is now etale. #suppress-qed
  ]
  Thus we have the equivalences of (1)(2)(3). 

  (1)(2)(3)$==>$(4): 

  #proof[
    We may assume that $Y = Spec(A)$ with $y$ corresponding to a prime $frak(p)$, and $X = Spec(B)$ with $x$ corresponding to $frak(q)$. By spreading out flatness, we assume that $B$ is flat over $A$. Put $ol(B) = B_(frak(p))\/frak(p) B_(frak(p))$ so that $X_y = Spec(ol(B))$ and let $ol(frak(q)) in Spec(ol(B))$ correspond to $frak(q)$. 

    Pick any presentation $B = P\/I, P = A[T_1, dots.c, T_n]$. Upon reduction to $ol(B)$, we have a presentation: 
    $
      ol(B) = k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_n]\/ol(I)
    $
    By @equiv-defns-of-smooth-auxiliary, up to suitably shrinking $Spec(B)$, we may assume that $ol(I) = (ol(F)_1, dots.c, ol(F)_(n-d))$ s.t. $ol(J) = display(mat(nabla ol(F)_1, dots.c, nabla ol(F)_(n-d)))$ has a $(n-d) times (n-d)$ minor invertible over $ol(B)$. WLOG we may assume that: 
    $
      det mat(display((partial ol(F)_j)/(partial T_i)))_(1<=i,j<=n-d) in ol(B)^times
    $
    Now consider the exact sequence $0 -> I -> P -> B -> 0$. Applying $- otimes_A k(y)$ gives: 
    $
      "Tor"_1^A (k(y), B) -> I_(frak(p))\/frak(p) I_(frak(p)) -> k(y)[T_1, dots.c, T_n] -> ol(B) -> 0
    $
    Now $B$ is flat over $A$, so the Tor term vanishes. Thus $ol(I) = I_(frak(p))\/frak(p) I_(frak(p))$. We may pick arbitrary lifts $F_i in I_(frak(p))$ of $ol(F)_i$, which must belong to $I_h$ for some $h in A$. Thus shrinking $A$, we may assume that we have lifts $F_i in I$ of $ol(F)_i$. Put $N = I\/(F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d))$, and let $frak(r) in Spec(P)$ correspond to $frak(q) in Spec(B)$. Then: 
    $
      N_(frak(r))\/frak(p) N_(frak(r)) = (N_(frak(p))\/frak(p) N_(frak(p)))_(frak(r)) = 0
    $ 
    since $ol(I) = (ol(F)_1, dots.c, ol(F)_(n-d))$ and so $I_(frak(p)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d)) + frak(p) I_(frak(p))$. Now $I$ is a finitely generated ideal, so $I$ is a finite $P$-module. Hence $N_(frak(r))$ is a finite $P_(frak(r))$-module, allowing Nakayama's lemma to be applied, showing $N_(frak(r)) = 0$, and so: 
    $
      I_(frak(r)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d))
    $
    Thus, after shrinking $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$ again, we may arrange lifts $F_i in I$ s.t. $I = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d))$. We know that: 
    $
      det mat(display((partial F_j)/(partial T_i)))_(1<=i,j<=n-d) mod frak(p) B_(frak(p)) = det mat(display((partial ol(F)_j)/(partial T_i)))_(1<=i,j<=n-d) in ol(B)^times
    $
    Therefore, at least $det display(mat((partial F_j)/(partial T_i))_(1<=i,j<=n-d)) in B$ is outside $frak(q)$. Shrink $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$ once more by inverting this determinant, we may assume that: 
    $
      det(H) in B^times, H := mat(display((partial F_j)/(partial T_i)))_(1<=i,j<=n-d)
    $
    and put $H^(-1) = display(mat(G_(i,j))) in GL_(n-d) (B)$. 

    Now consider the second exact sequence: 
    $
      I\/I^2 -> oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i -> Omega_(B\/A) -> 0
    $
    We make it split exact by constructing a left inverse of $delta: I\/I^2 -> oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i$. Indeed, put: 
    $
      lambda: oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i &-> I\/I^2 \
      d T_i &|-> sum_(j=1)^(n-d) G_(j,i) F_j, forall i <= n - d \
      d T_i &|-> 0, forall i > n-d
    $
    Then for $i <= n-d$: 
    $
      lambda(delta(F_i)) &= lambda(sum_(j=1)^(n-d) (partial F_i)/(partial T_j) d T_j) = sum_(j=1)^(n-d) sum_(k=1)^(n-d) (partial F_i)/(partial T_j) G_(j,k) F_k \
      &= sum_(k=1)^(n-d) F_k (sum_(j=1)^(n-d) (partial F_i)/(partial T_j) G_(k,j)) = sum_(k=1)^(n-d) F_k delta_(i,k) = F_i
    $
    We have thus split the exact sequence. 

    #suppress-qed
  ]
  
  (4)$==>$(5) is tautological, so it remains to prove (5)$==>$(3).  
  #proof[
    We may assume that $y$ corresponds to a prime $frak(p)$, and $x$ corresponds to $frak(q)$. In the presentation $B = P\/I$, $P = A[T_1,dots.c,T_n]$, let $frak(q)$ correspond to the prime $frak(r) in Spec(P)$. 
    
    Put $ol(B) = B_(frak(p))\/frak(p) B_(frak(p))$ so that $X_y = Spec(ol(B))$ and let $ol(frak(q)) in Spec(ol(B))$ correspond to $frak(q)$. 

    Spreading out the split exact sequence, we may assume that: 
    $
      0 -> I\/I^2 -> oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i -> Omega_(B\/A) -> 0
    $
    is split exact. Thus $I\/I^2$ and $Omega_(B\/A)$ are both locally free $B$-modules, and so shrinking $Spec(B)$ further, we may assume that they are both free of rank $n-d$ and $d$ respectively. Pick $F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d) in I$ that form a $B$-basis of $I\/I^2$. Now: 
    $
      I_(frak(r)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d)) + I_(frak(r))^2 subset (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d)) + frak(r) I_(frak(r))
    $
    By Nakayama's lemma, $I_(frak(r)) = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d))$. Thus shrinking $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$ more, we may assume that $I = (F_1, dots.c, F_(n-d))$. The map: 
    $
      delta: I\/I^2 = oplus.big_(i=1)^(n-d) B [F_i] &-> oplus.big_(i=1)^n B d T_i \
      F_i &|-> sum_(j=1)^n (partial F_i)/(partial T_j) d T_j
    $
    admits a left inverse. Thus the Jacobian matrix $J = display(mat(nabla F_1, dots.c, nabla F_(n-d)))$ admits a left inverse. Thus some $(n-d) times (n-d)$ minor of $J$ has determinant invertible in $k(x)$, i.e. in $B - frak(q)$. By shrinking $Spec(B)$ around $frak(q)$ again, we may assume that some $(n-d) times (n-d)$ minor of $J$ is invertible in $B$, say the first one. //By @jacobian-criterion-for-geom-regularity, $Spec(B) -> Spec(A)$ has geometrically regular fibers of dimension $d$. 
    
    We now factor: 
    $
      Spec(B) ->^j Spec(A[T_(n-d+1), dots.c, T_n]) -> Spec(A)
    $
    and aim to show that $j$ is etale. This follows from the Jacobian criterion for etaleness, @jacobian-criterion-for-etaleness. 
    #suppress-qed
  ]
  We have thereby proved the equivalence of (1)-(5). 
]

#definition(name: <defn-smooth-morphisms>)[
  If $f: X -> Y$ is locally of finite presentation, we define $f$ to be *smooth (of relative dimension $d$)* at $x$ if $f$ satisfies (1)-(5) above at $x$ (with the number $d$ as in condition (3)). 
  
  We say that $f$ is *smooth (of relative dimension $d$)*, if it is smooth (of relative dimension $d$) at every point of $X$. 
]

#properties[
  0. Smoothness (of relative dimension $d$) is Zariski local on source. 

  + A morphism is etale if and only if it is smooth of relative dimension $0$. 

  + Any base change of a smooth morphism of relative dimension $d$ is smooth of relative dimension $d$. 

  + If $f: X -> Y$ is smooth of relative dimension $d$, and $g: Y -> Z$ is smooth of relative dimension $e$, then $g compose f: X -> Z$ is smooth of relative dimension $d+e$. 
]
#proof[
  All of these properties follow from factorizations $X -> AA^d_Y -> Y$ with $X -> AA^d_Y$ etale. 
]
